// Returning a bool is the mistake C++ and Python invite -- a `<`-shaped
// comparison -- and one `cmp` returning an int is what keeps a total order
// from being defined inconsistently.
//
// The refusal lands on the DECLARATION, not on the `<` below. `cmp` is a
// reserved method name: the runtime calls it too, for `sort` and for a map
// key, and those calls are written nowhere in the program, so there is no
// use site to hang the message on. Checking the shape where it is written
// means a type with a wrong `cmp` cannot compile at all, rather than
// compiling until the first module that sorts it.
type P {
    int x;
}

bool P.cmp(P other) {
    return true;
}

P a = P(1);
P b = P(2);
print(a < b);
